  "response": "Sociologists approach the neighborhood question as a problem of causal identification rather than of portraiture, and the field's answer has been assembled out of successive designs, each imposing a stricter standard on how a family came to live where it lives. The earliest generation of this work was devoted to measuring the social properties of places and relating them to outcomes recorded in those same places. Robert Sampson's Project on Human Development in Chicago Neighborhoods measured collective efficacy across Chicago neighborhoods, and those measurements show that higher collective efficacy was associated with lower rates of neighborhood violence.\n\nPatrick Sharkey has documented multigenerational exposure to high-poverty neighborhoods among Black American families. That pattern of inherited place indicates that observational neighborhood studies are vulnerable to omitted variable bias from unmeasured parental traits, since the same families reappear in the same disadvantaged settings generation after generation. Precisely because unmeasured parental traits distort such estimates, Wodtke, Harding, and Elwert applied marginal structural models with inverse probability weighting to address time-varying confounding of neighborhood exposure. By contrast, sibling fixed-effects designs compare siblings whose childhoods were spent in neighborhoods of differing quality. Because every comparison is drawn within a single family, that design removes all unobserved confounding, including factors that change over time within a family.\n\nHousing policy supplied leverage of a different kind, since the allocation of a scarce benefit by lottery converts the question of selection into a question of who was offered a chance to move. The Gautreaux program relocated Chicago public housing residents under a court-ordered desegregation remedy, and the Moving to Opportunity demonstration was mounted afterward. That demonstration randomly assigned housing voucher offers to low-income families in five U.S. cities. Researchers used the randomized voucher offer as an instrument for subsequent exposure to neighborhood poverty. Chetty, Hendren, and Katz found that children who moved to lower-poverty neighborhoods before age 13 earned more as adults.\n\nThe administrative details of that demonstration govern what can be inferred from it. Moving to Opportunity recruited families in all fifty states through a national lottery, a recruitment arrangement that cannot be squared with the confinement of randomized voucher offers to five metropolitan areas. Its enrollment structure admits exactly one of two possibilities: either the demonstration included a randomly assigned control group that received no new housing voucher, or every family enrolled in Moving to Opportunity received an experimental housing voucher; one of the two held, and they cannot both have held. Adjudication therefore turns on a single criterion, namely whether a given design makes neighborhood exposure independent of the family characteristics that would otherwise produce the outcome under study.",
